      "instructions": "1-6: Complete the custom order form. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.\n7-10: Answer the questions. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.",
      "transcript": "Toby Langford: Good morning, Harbour Edge Framing. Toby speaking. How can I help?\nEliza Wren: Hello. I would like a quote for framing this print. I measured it at home, but perhaps I used the wrong edges.\nToby Langford: We measure the paper itself. Your enquiry says forty-five centimetres wide, but the print is forty-four. Shall I record forty-four?\nEliza Wren: Yes, please. Forty-five included the temporary border, which will be removed. The height is thirty centimetres.\nToby Langford: Now the mount. You ticked bright white, but beside this cream paper it looks cold. Warm white keeps the contrast gentle.\nEliza Wren: I agree. Please change the order to warm white. I considered pale grey as well, but it makes the sky in the print look dull.\nToby Langford: For the frame, we have pine, black aluminium and Tasmanian oak. The oak costs more, but its narrow profile suits the print.\nEliza Wren: Let us use Tasmanian oak. I expected to choose black aluminium, yet it feels too severe now that I can see it beside the picture.\nToby Langford: Would you like the timber left natural? We can also apply a charcoal finish or a light honey stain.\nEliza Wren: The honey stain is attractive, but the charcoal finish picks up the dark lines around the lighthouse. Charcoal is my final choice.\nToby Langford: Next is glazing. Ordinary glass is cheapest. Acrylic is lighter and safer in transport. Museum glass reduces reflections but raises the quote.\nEliza Wren: I carry pictures between my flat and my office, so low weight matters more than perfect reflection control. Use acrylic, not ordinary glass.\nToby Langford: The workshop needs nine days. Thursday the twenty-second of May would be possible late in the afternoon, but you said you work Thursdays.\nEliza Wren: Yes. Could I collect it on Friday the twenty-third instead? I can come before lunch.\nToby Langford: Good. The window mount will keep the print from touching the glazing. A decorative slip is not needed for separation.\nEliza Wren: Then no decorative slip. I would rather spend the money on suitable backing. What do you recommend?\nToby Langford: We use conservation board behind works on paper. Cardboard is cheaper, but it is not specified for this order.\nEliza Wren: Conservation board sounds sensible. I want the print protected, although it is not a valuable antique.\nToby Langford: Friday the twenty-third is available. Bring your order receipt; a photograph of it on your phone is acceptable. You will not need the card used for the deposit.\nEliza Wren: I will bring the order receipt. Is there anything I should know before I decide where to hang it?\nToby Langford: Avoid the wall above the radiator. Heat is unhelpful for paper. The interior wall near your bookcase sounded better than the sunny wall.\nEliza Wren: All right, not above the radiator and not beside the sunny window. Please include the hanging wire and hooks in the quote.\nToby Langford: The wire and two standard hooks are included. The total also includes the fitting fee, so there will not be a compulsory workshop charge added at collection.\nEliza Wren: Let me check the order: forty-four centimetres wide, warm white mount, Tasmanian oak with a charcoal finish, acrylic glazing, and collection on the twenty-third of May.\nToby Langford: Correct. The window mount and conservation board provide the protective layers. Bring the receipt on Friday, and we will show you how the two hooks should be spaced.",
