      "title": "Listening — Part 3: Educational discussion: two trainee mediators revise a housing-dispute role-play in Belfast",
      "instructions": "21-25: Choose the correct purpose, A-G, for each coded stage on the trainees’ worksheet.\n26-30: Complete the sentences. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.",
      "transcript": "Orla Devlin: That role-play stalled earlier than I expected. Once the neighbours began arguing about the late-night music, we jumped straight to solutions.\nFinn Hollis: Yes, and our opening was rushed. Let us rebuild the sequence before the next training session, starting with the purpose of each stage.\nOrla Devlin: The worksheet calls the opening statement Stage R. Here we outline the process, confidentiality and our impartial role, rather than judging whose evidence is stronger.\nFinn Hollis: Each person’s account is Stage T. We first hear events as that person understands them, saving our follow-up questions until the account has finished.\nOrla Devlin: The issue summary is Stage K. It establishes the topics needing discussion. In our case, those include music, hallway use and messages through the landlord.\nFinn Hollis: Interest exploration is Stage P. We look for practical needs behind stated demands, such as sleep, privacy, predictability or feeling respected.\nOrla Devlin: Option generation is Stage M. The parties suggest possible approaches, while we postpone any judgment of those ideas until the next stage.\nFinn Hollis: Evaluation and agreement come later. We confused those stages by rejecting suggestions as soon as they were spoken.\nOrla Devlin: So the worksheet codes are clear: R introduces the process, T hears each account, K sets the topics, P uncovers needs, and M gathers ideas.\nFinn Hollis: We also need to revise our wording. I told them to bring proof of every complaint, which made the session sound like a hearing.\nOrla Devlin: Let us call photographs, notes and messages information rather than proof. They can help clarify events, but we are not deciding a legal case.\nFinn Hollis: Agreed. I also proposed a ground rule saying they should use surnames. That felt formal and one participant kept forgetting it.\nOrla Devlin: They had already asked to use first names, so we should respect that preference. The useful rule is to speak to one another without insults.\nFinn Hollis: What about the documents from the landlord? I thought we should verify them before adding anything to the agenda.\nOrla Devlin: We can acknowledge them, but the dispute about noise belongs on the shared agenda whether or not a document settles the date of one incident.\nFinn Hollis: Right. We are mapping what needs discussion, not ruling on admissibility. The hallway issue also goes on that list because both people raised it.\nOrla Devlin: During interest exploration, I asked, Who is right about quiet hours? That invited another argument over positions.\nFinn Hollis: A better question is, What matters most about the evenings at home? One neighbour may say sleep, while the other may mention having friends visit.\nOrla Devlin: We can follow with questions about patterns and impact. We should avoid suggesting that either concern automatically outweighs the other.\nFinn Hollis: For option generation, shall I write every suggestion on the whiteboard? It was difficult to keep the issue summary visible last time.\nOrla Devlin: Use a separate sheet for options. Then the agreed agenda remains visible, and we can group similar ideas after the brainstorming stage.\nFinn Hollis: Good. We should also tell participants that listing an option does not mean accepting it. Otherwise they may refuse to suggest anything flexible.\nOrla Devlin: Exactly. Last time one neighbour proposed headphones, and the other heard that as a final demand. We need to mark it as an idea for later evaluation.\nFinn Hollis: Another mistake was my summary. I said one neighbour wanted silence, but she had actually asked for predictable quiet after eleven.\nOrla Devlin: That distinction is an underlying interest. Absolute silence is a position; reliable rest is the concern we can explore alongside the other person’s social needs.\nFinn Hollis: Should we set a time limit for each uninterrupted account? Five minutes felt short, but ten allowed repeated accusations.\nOrla Devlin: We can say about seven minutes and give a neutral reminder if someone repeats. The exact timing is a training choice, not a rule for every mediation.\nFinn Hollis: At the end, any agreement must use the parties’ own decisions. We can test whether a proposal is clear and workable without drafting a solution for them.\nOrla Devlin: And if they do not agree, we still close by summarising what was discussed and any points of progress. A session is not automatically a failure because no full settlement is reached.\nFinn Hollis: Let me note our revisions: information, first names, noise on the shared agenda, a question about what matters most, and a separate sheet for options.\nOrla Devlin: That should keep our role clear. We guide the sequence and communication, while the neighbours remain responsible for their choices.",
