      "title": "Listening — Part 4: Academic monologue: hummingbird torpor in a zoology lecture in Ottawa",
      "instructions": "31-36: Complete the notes. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.\n37-40: Answer the questions. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.",
      "transcript": "Lecturer: Today we will examine hummingbird torpor, a controlled reduction in physiological activity during the night.\nLecturer: Hummingbirds spend energy rapidly. Small bodies lose heat quickly, and hovering is expensive, so hours without feeding create an energy problem.\nLecturer: Before night, the amount of stored fuel matters. Researchers often discuss energy reserves when asking whether a bird enters torpor and how long it remains there.\nLecturer: This is not a switch determined by one measurement. Species, condition, feeding and weather may all influence the pattern.\nLecturer: During torpor, metabolic rate falls substantially. The bird uses energy more slowly than it would while maintaining its usual active temperature.\nLecturer: Body temperature also declines. In deeper torpor it may approach ambient temperature, although it remains physiologically regulated rather than becoming an uncontrolled response to cold.\nLecturer: The immediate benefit is energy savings. A bird can preserve fuel through a long night instead of spending continuously to keep its daytime temperature.\nLecturer: Depth and duration vary. Torpor is not identical in every individual or every night.\nLecturer: Entry is gradual. As evening activity ends, heat production declines and temperature follows. A lower overnight set point reduces the difference between the body and the surrounding air.\nLecturer: That smaller temperature difference reduces heat loss, which contributes to the saving. It does not mean the environment supplies the bird with energy.\nLecturer: Near dawn, the bird must rewarm. Muscle activity produces much of the necessary heat, and this process consumes part of the fuel conserved overnight.\nLecturer: Rewarming is therefore a cost, not free recovery. Its timing matters because a bird that becomes active early may reach flowers before competitors, but it also begins spending energy sooner.\nLecturer: Once active, feeding can restore reserves. Early access to nectar may be important, especially after a cold or lengthy night.\nLecturer: We can now separate the sequence: available reserves help shape entry, metabolic rate and body temperature fall, energy is conserved, muscle activity supports rewarming, and feeding follows renewed activity.\nLecturer: Let us turn to ecological variation. Night length can extend the period during which no food is available. Longer darkness can therefore increase the value of conserving energy.\nLecturer: Air temperature also changes the energetic challenge. A colder environment generally increases the cost of keeping a warm body above its surroundings.\nLecturer: Food conditions matter as well. A bird that accumulated ample reserves may use torpor differently from one that ended the day with less stored fuel.\nLecturer: Researchers test rather than assume these relationships. They measure body mass, record temperature, and identify each torpor bout.\nLecturer: One Andean study compared hummingbirds across elevations. Higher and lower sites differed in climate and ecological setting, allowing researchers to examine variation without claiming that elevation was the only cause.\nLecturer: The study also considered species and individual differences. A pattern observed at one site may reflect the birds present there as well as the physical environment.\nLecturer: Another line of research asks whether a threshold of stored energy helps explain torpor use. The idea is useful because it produces predictions, but a simple threshold may not capture every bout.\nLecturer: Duration is especially informative. Two birds may both enter torpor, yet one remains in that state much longer. Counting entry alone would miss that distinction.\nLecturer: Researchers must also distinguish correlation from mechanism. If cold nights coincide with longer torpor, temperature is associated with the pattern, but food availability or night length may also contribute.\nLecturer: Controlled measurements clarify physiology, while field observations preserve ecological realism. Strong accounts combine both.\nLecturer: What does this mean for broad claims? First, do not describe torpor as an emergency response used only when a bird is nearly starving.\nLecturer: Some hummingbirds may use it strategically under conditions where conserving energy improves the overnight budget. That does not prove deliberate conscious planning.\nLecturer: Second, avoid species-wide conclusions from one population. Hummingbirds occupy diverse habitats, and closely related species can show different patterns.\nLecturer: Third, report what was actually measured. Skin temperature, body temperature, metabolic rate and movement are related indicators, but they are not interchangeable.\nLecturer: A temperature trace can identify cooling and rewarming, while oxygen consumption offers evidence about metabolism. Field equipment may make one measure easier than another.\nLecturer: Finally, remember the cost after torpor. Rewarming uses energy, and delayed activity may alter feeding opportunities. Energy saved overnight is only one side of the budget.\nLecturer: The central lesson is that torpor links physiology with ecology. Reserves and environmental conditions shape entry and duration; reduced metabolism and temperature save fuel; rewarming and renewed feeding complete the cycle.\nLecturer: When reading a study, ask which birds were measured, at what elevation, during which season, and with which physiological indicator. Those limits determine how far the conclusion can travel.\nLecturer: Torpor is remarkable, but the scientific explanation is strongest when it preserves variation instead of replacing it with a single dramatic rule.",
