      "passage": "A In dry places, a cloud touching a hillside can carry water past communities that receive little rain. Fog collectors intercept some of that moving moisture without pumps or refrigeration. Their apparent simplicity is deceptive: a panel of netting does not pull vapour from clear air. It works when suspended liquid droplets in fog strike its surface. Output therefore depends on weather, landscape and engineering together. A successful installation must face suitable fog, admit enough air, retain droplets and move the collected water away before wind returns it to the atmosphere. The device is best understood as a chain whose weakest link limits the final yield. Because the system has no powered condenser, it may be cheap to run, but it cannot operate on demand. Storage must bridge the intervals between productive fog events, and users need a separate plan when the season disappoints. Even a well-sited panel must therefore be judged across many events and against the community’s demand.\n\nB A large collector is a rectangular frame anchored against strong winds. Across its top, a support cable keeps the capture surface under tension. The main surface is often a double-layer mesh; a narrow layer gap separates the two sheets. Its crossing threads form mesh junctions. The first retained droplets lodge at mesh junctions. There, minute beads meet beads and become larger drops. Once heavy enough, they descend into a collection gutter fixed below the panel. The gutter slopes towards a storage pipe, which carries water to a covered tank. These parts form a continuous route: interception above, enlargement within the net, drainage below and protected transfer beyond the frame.\n\nC The first stage is collision. A droplet following moving air may be intercepted instead of curving around the net. Very small droplets tend to follow the stream, whereas larger ones have more inertia; however, a mesh cannot simply be made solid to catch everything. If its open spaces are too restricted, airflow is diverted around the whole panel. After impact, surface properties determine whether water spreads, remains attached or is blown off. Droplets that stay can merge, and gravity then competes successfully with adhesion, drawing them downward. Efficient collection thus requires both capture and release. A surface that holds every bead indefinitely would be as unhelpful as one from which beads rebound immediately.\n\nD Placement begins with observation rather than construction. Surveyors examine fog frequency, seasonal timing and the direction from which local winds arrive during fog events. A ridge may appear cloudy yet perform poorly if turbulence sends air over and around a proposed panel. Nearby vegetation, rock faces and buildings can also alter the flow. Collectors are normally set across the incoming wind, but the best orientation is a local finding, not a universal compass bearing. Height matters too: a panel close to rough ground can sit in disturbed air, while an exposed crest may impose damaging loads. Pilot collectors are valuable because measured water integrates these factors more reliably than a single visual inspection.\n\nE Mesh design involves another compromise. More filament offers more targets, but less open area can reduce the volume of fog passing through. Two nets with the same nominal density may behave differently because thread width, weave, flexibility and spacing affect both airflow and drainage. A second layer can increase interception, yet the gap between layers must not trap water where it can be re-entrained. Engineers therefore compare designs under the same conditions rather than attributing all variation to material alone. Reported daily yields also need context: a high figure from a brief dense-fog episode cannot describe an entire year, and an average from one ridge should not be transferred automatically to another settlement.\n\nF Operation continues after installation. Dust can coat threads, leaves can block the gutter, and ultraviolet exposure or repeated flexing can weaken netting. Regular inspection removes obstructions and finds tears before they spread. Teams may keep spare mesh on site because a delayed repair during the fog season can forfeit much of the annual supply. Tanks and pipes also require hygienic protection; collecting atmospheric droplets does not by itself guarantee drinkable water. Most importantly, planners should describe fog collection as a supplementary supply where conditions justify it, not as a universal replacement for wells or networks. Modest technology still needs local measurements, secure maintenance responsibility and a plan for years when fog is scarce.",
