      "passage": "A For centuries, mariners blamed a 'worm' for timber that failed below the waterline. The name survives, but shipworms are not worms. They are highly modified bivalve molluscs related to clams. Their elongated bodies occupy tunnels in wood, while a pair of very small shells at the front works as a cutting tool. At the other end, siphons reach the surrounding water for respiration and feeding. This unusual anatomy explains why observers classified the animal by its damage long before its relationships were clear. It also explains a persistent historical problem: the destructive organism could remain largely concealed while a hull or harbour pile appeared sound from outside.\n\nB Attack begins when free-swimming larvae settle on suitable submerged timber. After entry, the young animal enlarges a tunnel as it grows, scraping wood with its shells and lining the passage with a thin calcareous layer. Only a small opening for the siphons may remain visible at the surface. Separate tunnels can curve around one another, leaving fragile partitions between them. A heavily occupied beam may consequently keep its external shape even after much internal wood has disappeared. Sudden breakage is therefore not necessarily evidence of sudden attack. It can be the delayed mechanical result of many hidden galleries created over time, a mismatch between visible appearance and remaining strength.\n\nC Shipworm presence is not uniform. Species differ in their environmental tolerances, and both salinity and temperature can limit survival, reproduction or spread. Fresh water excludes many marine wood-borers, which is why river discharge may offer some structures temporary protection. Yet the boundary is neither identical for every species nor fixed through the year. Drought can allow saltier water to move farther into an estuary, while cold periods may slow one population more than another. Historical reports must therefore be read alongside local conditions. The absence of damage in one season does not establish permanent safety, and a defence successful in a cool, brackish harbour may fail after environmental conditions change.\n\nD Wooden shipping also gave these animals a means of travel. Adults living in a hull could release larvae during a voyage or after arrival, and infested timbers could be moved between waterfronts. Expanding trade thus connected habitats that had previously been separated, although reconstructing each introduction is difficult. Old records often use broad names, and damaged wood may have been replaced without scientific identification. Modern surveys can establish present distributions and genetic relationships, but they cannot supply an exact arrival date for every historical population. The strongest account therefore combines biological plausibility with shipping records while acknowledging gaps, rather than turning every new port record into proof of one particular voyage.\n\nE Evidence about hazard changed questions for ports. Because destructive capacity varies among species and environments, wooden maritime construction cannot be managed as one uniform problem. The survey identifies the species posing the greatest danger and recommends monitoring them where wood is extensively used in maritime construction. That response links biological evidence to port maintenance: managers must identify the organisms present, observe local temperature and salinity, and inspect vulnerable timber rather than assume that one design decision provides permanent protection. Construction practice therefore becomes iterative. Materials and exposed members are assessed against local risk, and new observations can alter inspection priorities without claiming that a survey predicts every harbour or season.\n\nF The resulting pattern was uneven. Teredinids occurred at twenty-four of thirty-two surveyed sites, while none recruited to panels at seven sites. Nine species were considered established in European coastal waters, and their ranges were not identical. Some appeared across regions; others within narrower areas. The study related these distributions to temperature and salinity, then identified species whose destructive capacity warranted future field monitoring. A survey could not turn this evidence into a permanent map. Records differed by date, duration and sampling method, while environmental limits varied among species. For harbour managers, the lesson was to assess hazard by place and organism, rather than infer one fixed risk from the general label shipworm alone.\n\nG Today, old submerged timber poses a double question. Archaeologists may value a wreck, quay or defence as evidence, yet raising wood can expose it to decay processes different from those underwater. Leaving it in place may avoid disturbance but does not protect it automatically from wood-borers. Experiments that place standard wooden panels near a site can reveal current attack pressure, while salinity and temperature measurements help interpret the results. Even so, a test panel is a sample, not the monument itself. Conservation decisions must balance access, disturbance, cost and uncertainty. The history of shipworms is thus not a simple contest with a victorious invention; it is a record of repeated adaptation as organisms, shipping networks, materials and human priorities changed.",
